{
  "stage": "extract",
  "item": "2r0QahNdwMw",
  "inputs_hash": "a79855a348c99fad99edc9208aca1ca614d6edabc5a3d71ce8f05b2bbca1332d",
  "output_hash": "c8ac9079c7bcc6da5d0bd6e0d23d6f95a7e528101cd0ddac1aad92eaf6cb85ac",
  "output": {
    "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
    "points": [
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p001",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "Shortcut connections are an important building block of the GPT architecture, alongside layer normalization, the GELU activation, and the feed-forward neural network.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@23",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@26",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@28",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@30",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@32",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@35"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p002",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "In the Transformer block, the plus symbols and their associated arrows represent shortcut connections.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@96",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@98",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@100",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@103",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@105",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@108",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@110",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@112",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@114",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@117",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@120",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@122",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@124",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@126",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@129",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@130",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@132"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p003",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Shortcut connections are also known as skip connections or residual connections.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@150",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@153",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@155",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@157",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@158",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@160",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@162",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@164"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p004",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "Shortcut connections were first proposed in computer vision to solve the vanishing gradient problem.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@167",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@169",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@172",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@174",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@178",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@180"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p005",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The vanishing gradient problem is that gradients become progressively smaller as they are propagated backward through a neural network.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@194",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@195",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@198",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@200"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p006",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "When gradients become very small, weight updates are not made, learning becomes stagnant, convergence is delayed, and the language model does not learn well.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@200",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@202",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@205",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@207",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@210",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@212"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p007",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "During backpropagation, gradient flow goes from the output of the neural network to the input, so later-layer gradients are used to compute earlier-layer gradients.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@252",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@254",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@256",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@258",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@260",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@262",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@265",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@267",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@269",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@271",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@273"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p008",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Because backpropagation involves multiplications, if a later gradient becomes small then earlier gradients become even smaller, which is why gradients can vanish.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@273",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@275",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@277",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@279",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@282",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@286",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@288",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@290",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@292",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@295",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@297",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@299",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@302",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@304",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@306"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p009",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "equation",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The weight update rule is that the new weight equals the old weight minus the step size times the partial derivative of the loss with respect to the weight.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@323",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@324",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@327",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@329",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@331",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@333",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@336",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@339",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@343",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@346"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p010",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "If the gradient of the loss with respect to a weight approaches zero, the weight is not updated, so the neural network is not learning.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@349",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@351",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@353",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@355",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@357",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@362",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@364",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@366",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@368",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@370",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@373",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@375",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@376"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p011",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The vanishing gradient problem leads to stagnation, and shortcut connections solve this problem.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@378",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@381",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@382",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@385",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@388",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@391",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@393"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p012",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Shortcut connections create an alternative path for the gradient to flow by skipping one or more layers.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@395",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@398",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@401",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@403",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@407",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@409"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p013",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "definition",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "A shortcut connection is implemented by adding the output of one layer to the output of a later layer.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@412",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@415",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@418",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@508",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@510",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@512",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@515",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@517",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@519",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@521",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@524",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@526",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@528",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@530"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p014",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "visual_ref",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "In the example network without shortcut connections, the displayed gradient magnitudes become much smaller in earlier layers, illustrating the vanishing gradient problem.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@421",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@425",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@427",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@429",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@431",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@438",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@441",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@443",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@444",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@448",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@450",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@452",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@454",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@457",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@459",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@462",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@465",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@467",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@469",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@472",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@474",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@475"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p015",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "visual_ref",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "In the example network with shortcut connections, the displayed gradient magnitude in the first layer is much larger than without shortcut connections, indicating that vanishing gradients are reduced.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@477",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@479",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@482",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@485",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@487",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@488",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@491",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@493",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@495",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@496",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@499",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@501",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@503",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@505",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@506",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@533",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@535",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@537",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@541",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@545",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@549",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@550",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@552",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@554",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@555",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@558",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@560",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@562",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@563",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@567"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p016",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "equation",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "With a shortcut connection between two layers, the output of the later layer is the function output plus the earlier layer output, that is, y_(l+1) = f(y_l) + y_l.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@599",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@602",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@604",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@606",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@608",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@610",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@612",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@615",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@617",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@620",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@622",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@624",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@626",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@629",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@630",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@662",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@664",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@667",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@670",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@672",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@673",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@677",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@679",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@683"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p017",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "To prevent vanishing gradients, the partial derivative of the loss with respect to the first layer output should stay as large as possible so that the gradients with respect to the first layer weights do not go to zero.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@683",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@685",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@687",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@688",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@691",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@693",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@694",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@696",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@698",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@701",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@703",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@705",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@707",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@709",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@711",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@713",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@715",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@717"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p018",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "equation",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Using the chain rule, the partial derivative of the loss with respect to y_l equals the partial derivative of the loss with respect to y_(l+1) multiplied by the partial derivative of y_(l+1) with respect to y_l.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@719",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@721",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@724",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@725",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@728",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@729",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@732",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@734"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p019",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "equation",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Because y_(l+1) = f(y_l) + y_l, the derivative of y_(l+1) with respect to y_l includes the derivative of f(y_l) plus 1.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@734",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@739",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@741",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@743",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@746",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@748"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p020",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "The plus one term introduced by the shortcut connection keeps the gradient from vanishing even if the derivative through the main path becomes small.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@767",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@770",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@773",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@774",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@776",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@778",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@780",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@782",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@785",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@788",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@790",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@793",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@795",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@796",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@799",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@800",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@802",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@804",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@806",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@809"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p021",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Because the shortcut connection keeps gradients flowing, the loss gradients with respect to earlier-layer weights do not ultimately vanish, so training does not stagnate.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@813",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@815",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@818",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@820",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@822",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@824",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@826",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@828",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@830",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@832",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@835",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@837",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@839",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@841",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@843",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@845",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@847",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@849"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p022",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Shortcut connections play a crucial role during backpropagation by preserving gradient flow through the network.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@863",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@865",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@867",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@869",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@871",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@873",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@874",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@878",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@879",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@882",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@884",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@887",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@889"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p023",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "visual_ref",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "The shown loss-landscape comparison suggests that without skip connections there are many local minima, peaks, and valleys, whereas with skip connections the landscape appears smoother with fewer local minima.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@892",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@894",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@897",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@900",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@902",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@904",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@907",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@908",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@911",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@913",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@915",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@917",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@920",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@922"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p024",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "claim",
        "importance": 3,
        "text": "Shortcut connections smooth the loss-function landscape and make gradient flow smoother.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@925",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@927",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@928",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@931",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@942",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@944",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@946",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@948",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@949",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@952"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "2r0QahNdwMw:p025",
        "video_id": "2r0QahNdwMw",
        "lecture": 22,
        "kind": "visual_ref",
        "importance": 2,
        "text": "The lecturer shows a comparison in which the loss-function landscape without skip connections has many oscillations, while the landscape with skip connections is much smoother.",
        "line_ids": [
          "2r0QahNdwMw@937",
          "2r0QahNdwMw@938",
